# Text Hierarchy

Typography establishes information hierarchy before color, spacing, or visual decoration.

Each level of text should communicate its relative importance through consistent size, weight, and placement.

Users should immediately distinguish between:

- Page titles
- Section headings
- Card titles
- Body content
- Supporting information
- Metadata

Hierarchy should remain predictable across every module.

---

# Readability First

Typography should always be optimized for reading.

Avoid:

Dense paragraphs

Tiny text

Overly bold interfaces

Poor line spacing

Reading should feel effortless.

---

# Body Text

Default body size

16px

Body text should remain comfortable across desktop and mobile.

Avoid reducing body text below 14px.

Body text should remain the default reading experience throughout the application.

---

# Headings

Headings communicate structure—not decoration.

Recommended hierarchy

H1

Primary page title

H2

Major section

H3

Card title

H4

Small subsection

Avoid skipping heading levels.

---

# Font Weight

Use weight intentionally.

Recommended

Regular

400

Medium

500

Semibold

600

Bold

700

Avoid excessive bold text.

If everything is bold, nothing stands out.

Avoid using multiple font weights within the same visual hierarchy unless they communicate different semantic importance.

---

# Emphasis

Prefer hierarchy before weight.

Increase emphasis using:

Spacing

Position

Size

Color

Weight

Bold should be the final tool—not the first.

---

# Line Height

Readable interfaces require generous spacing.

Recommended

Headings

1.2–1.3

Body

1.5–1.7

Small labels

1.3–1.4

Avoid cramped text.

---

# Letter Spacing

Use default spacing unless a design token specifies otherwise.

Avoid manually adjusting tracking across the interface.

Consistency is more important than experimentation.

---

# Line Length

Readable paragraphs should remain narrow.

Ideal

45–75 characters

Avoid full-width paragraphs on desktop.

Use max-width constraints for long-form content.

---

# Text Alignment

Default alignment

Left

Avoid centered paragraphs.

Center alignment should be reserved for:

Landing pages

Empty states

Success screens

Reading should remain predictable.

---

# Text Color Hierarchy

Use semantic colors.

Primary text

Highest emphasis

Secondary text

Supporting information

Muted text

Metadata

Disabled text

Unavailable information

Never use multiple shades randomly.

---

# Numeric Data

Numbers require consistency.

Use tabular numerals whenever possible.

Examples

Revenue

Occupancy

Payments

Statistics

Tables

Dashboards

Aligned numbers improve readability.

---

# Monospace Usage

JetBrains Mono should only appear when:

IDs

Invoice numbers

Room codes

API keys

Reference values

Logs

Never use monospace for navigation or descriptive content.

---

# Responsive Typography

Scale typography naturally.

Do not simply shrink every heading.

Prioritize readability across:

Desktop

Tablet

Mobile

Typography should adapt to available space while preserving the established hierarchy.

---

# Consistency

Typography should be predictable.

Users should immediately recognize:

Titles

Sections

Metadata

Descriptions

Actions

Never invent new styles when existing ones satisfy the requirement.

---

# Typography Governance

The typography system should evolve intentionally.

Before introducing a new typography pattern, evaluate:

- Can an existing text style solve the problem?
- Does the new style improve readability?
- Will it remain consistent across modules?
- Does it increase unnecessary complexity?

Typography should evolve through refinement rather than introducing additional text styles.

Avoid creating one-off typography variants for individual screens.

Consistency should always take precedence over stylistic preference.

---

# Typography Quality Gates

The typography system remains healthy when:

- Font families remain consistent.
- Type scale is applied consistently.
- Information hierarchy is immediately understandable.
- Text remains readable across supported devices.
- Numeric information remains easy to compare.
- Typography supports accessibility requirements.
- New typography styles are introduced intentionally.
- Typography remains aligned with the Design Standards.
- Typography hierarchy remains consistent across modules.
- Typography supports efficient information scanning.

This document defines the typography standards for the hubIO platform.

Implementation guidance is defined by the Engineering Constitution and the relevant Engineering Standards.